.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1236 PM PDT Mon Oct 5 2026
(This evening through Tuesday night)

Stratus clouds lurk at the immediate coast, associated with a
shallow marine layer around 500-1000 feet thick, but the rest of the
region remains hot through the next couple of days as ridging
persists across the western United States, ridging which is helping
compress the marine layer and preventing any substantial overnight
relief from reaching the higher elevations. To our northeast, high
level clouds and convective remnants, associated with a puff of
moisture riding an embedded low in the larger scale ridging, linger
over the northern Sierra Nevada range and into the northern sections
of the Great Basin. This time yesterday, that puff of moisture was
streaming over the Bay Area, resulting in streaming high clouds that
contributed to some cooler (relatively speaking) conditions than the
expectation. Today the skies remain clear, and it`s possible for
today`s high temperatures to overperform yesterday`s, especially
across the North Bay. High temperatures today and Tuesday range from
the middle 90s to near 106 across the inland valleys, the middle 80s
to the lower 90s near the Bays, and the upper 60s to the lower 70s
along the Pacific coast. Mild overnight lows in the higher
elevations, ranging from the upper 60s to the middle 70s, along with
weak onshore flow will limit overnight recoveries.

A Heat Advisory remains in effect for the interior regions,
excluding the coastal strip and the northern Salinas Valley, as
widespread Moderate HeatRisk blankets the interior, corresponding to
a moderate risk of heat-related illnesses for sensitive populations,
including children, the elderly, pregnant women, people with chronic
illnesses, and people who work or live outdoors without access to
adequate cooling or hydration. In addition, many of the higher
elevations are seeing Major HeatRisk, corresponding to a high risk
for heat-related illnesses for anyone without hydration or cooling.
Follow these tips to avoid heat exhaustion and heatstroke:

* Stay hydrated and drink plenty of fluids.
* Wear lightweight, light-colored clothing.
* Reduce time spent outdoors or stay in the shade.
* Never leave people or pets unattended in vehicles.
* Use sunscreen if going to the coast or the pool.

