.AVIATION...
(18Z TAFS)
Issued at 1047 AM PDT Mon Oct 5 2026

A shallow marine layer (around 500 feet in depth) has returned
allowing for coastal stratus and fog to return to the coastal
sites. As surface temperatures warm, expecting mostly VFR
conditions to persist through the early evening before a return of
stratus and/or fog returns to coastal sites late this evening and
into Tuesday morning. The greatest potential for sub-IFR will be
at HAF, MRY, and SNS with the rest of the region remaining VFR
throughout the TAF period. Onshore winds will increase today and
be slightly stronger than previous days before easing after sunset
and more so into Tuesday morning. Any low ceilings and/or fog are
likely to dissipate between 16Z-17Z Tuesday.

Vicinity of SFO...VFR. Onshore winds will increase by late
morning and persist into early evening before diminishing. West to
northwest winds will be strongest this afternoon through mid-
evening with winds forecast to be up to 16kt. While there is a low
probability (generally 20-25%) of sub-MVFR from 12Z-17Z Tuesday,
have these out of the forecast at this time.

SFO Bridge Approach...Similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals...VFR. Onshore winds will increase this
afternoon before diminishing after sunset. Sub-IFR conditions are
forecast to return to the region late this evening and persist
into Tuesday morning with generally moderate to high confidence of
occurrence. Low ceiling and fog will begin to dissipate between
16Z- 17Z Tuesday with afternoon heating.

